\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\insrsid7866092\charrsid7866092 \hich\af0\dbch\af31505\loch\f0 Impact of female land ownership on adoption of improved agricultural }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\insrsid7866092 
\par }\pard \ltrpar\qj \li0\ri0\sa160\sl278\slmult1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid9785 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\insrsid7866092 \hich\af0\dbch\af31505\loch\f0 
Female land ownership is taken as a proxy for participation of women in household decision making\hich\af0\dbch\af31505\loch\f0 
 and its impact of improved agricultural practices like the use of chemical fertilizer, crop rotation practices and use of improved maize variety was studied.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\insrsid1836154 \hich\af0\dbch\af31505\loch\f0 
 Soil infertility level was used as qualitative control for land type. Drought shock was used to control for climate faced. Age, education, gender of household head, wealth index,}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\insrsid9785 
\hich\af0\dbch\af31505\loch\f0  possession of non-farm enterprise, and dependency ratio was used to control for household characteristics. D}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\insrsid1836154 \hich\af0\dbch\af31505\loch\f0 
